R"VG(
# futuristic2.vg (H-04; the second pass 2026-10-10): the lesson (a teacher's record for children: their world, their ways and an event
# of their own history, `@event`, futuristic7.vg's), the assembly's vote, the code of law. A late people keeps many lessons and votes:
# every part is composed of the people's own world (its sky, its land, its crops and herds, its feasts and crafts, its words).

@root lesson.*

# ---- the lesson ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.lesson (words=47..185)
  {ls.head}\n{ls.world}\n{@event founding,built,invention,discovery,war,battle,siege,sack,peace,sickness,famine,flood,drought,quake,eruption,impact,flare,winter,institution,government,accession,faith,deed,unrest,migration,abandoned,contact,treaty,otherend,omen,way,fire,storm,accident,wonder,games,law,trial,embassy,show,stones,coming,first,death lesson 3000}\n{ls.ask}
  {ls.head}\n{@event founding,built,invention,discovery,war,battle,siege,sack,peace,sickness,famine,flood,drought,quake,eruption,impact,flare,winter,institution,government,accession,faith,deed,unrest,migration,abandoned,contact,treaty,otherend,omen,way,fire,storm,accident,wonder,games,law,trial,embassy,show,stones,coming,first,death lesson 3000}\n{ls.world}\n{ls.ask}
  {ls.head}\n{ls.world}\n{@event founding,built,invention,discovery,war,battle,siege,sack,peace,sickness,famine,flood,drought,quake,eruption,impact,flare,winter,institution,government,accession,faith,deed,unrest,migration,abandoned,contact,treaty,otherend,omen,way,fire,storm,accident,wonder,games,law,trial,embassy,show,stones,coming,first,death lesson 3000}\n{ls.moral} {ls.ask}

ls.head (formula people=4)
  A lesson for the children of {$home}, recorded by their teacher.
  Today's lesson, for the archive.
  (has:$f) A lesson given at {$home}; the teacher's notes.
  The {#n:ord} lesson of {season.now}, {$home}. For the archive.
  Lesson, {$home}, cycle {#year:digits}. {#n} children, {#n} of them listening.
  For the archive: what I taught today at {$home}, and how it went.
  The children of the {fut.block} at {$home}: today's lesson.

ls.world
  (2) {ls.fact} {ls.fact}
  (2) {ls.fact} {ls.fact} {ls.fact}
  (1) We began outside. {ls.fact} {ls.fact}

ls.fact
  (2 once) {ls.sky}
  (2 once) {ls.land}
  (2 once) {ls.life}
  (2 once) {ls.ways}
  (1 once) {ls.word}
  (1 once) {ls.past}

# what is over them
ls.sky (share=0.7)
  Our world turns once a day, as you know, and goes round {sun.first} once a year.
  {sun.first} is a star like the others, only near; the others are suns too, and some of their worlds may grow {crop}, for all we know.
  (moons>0) {moon} is our moon, older than the {tree} on {mountain}, and it will be here after us.
  (moons>1) We have {#moons} moons. The old {people} named each of them, and argued about the names.
  (moons=0) We have no moon. Other worlds do; we have {star} instead, which never moves.
  (rings=1) The ring is ice and stone, very thin, and very wide. From the side it nearly disappears.
  (aurora=1) The lights in the north come from {sun.first}, through the air, when it is restless.
  (comet=1) The hairy star comes back. The old records counted its returns before they knew why.
  (giant=1) The wanderer is a world much bigger than ours, made mostly of air.
  (companion=1) The second sun is a star of its own. Our world goes round only one of them.
  (locked=1) One side of our world always faces {sun.first}; we live where the day meets the night.
  (tilted=1) The seasons come because our world leans. In {season.now} our half leans {ls.lean}.
  (longnight=1) Our nights are long because our world turns slowly. The {people} learned to sleep twice.
  The stars the old {people} called the {herd} are not near each other at all; they only look it from here.
  The old {people} named the stars after the {herd}; we still call one of them the shepherd.
  {sun.first} rises over {mountain} at {festival}, and the old {people} built their doors to face it and painted them {colour.holy}.
  (moons>0) The old ones planted the {crop} by {moon}; some of the gardeners still do.
  At night the sky over {$home} turns, slowly; the old ones said it was {god} walking the {herd} home.
  The light from {star} left it before the first {people} drove their {herd} here.

# what is under them
ls.land (share=0.6)
  {river} rises under {mountain} and runs a long way to the sea, and people have grown {crop} by it since there were people.
  (desert=1) The sand was once sea. You can find shells in the dunes if you look.
  (desert=1) There was rain here once, every year. The old records complain about it.
  (desert=0) The {tree} on the hills are older than the oldest house in {$home}.
  (desert=0 coast=1) The tide comes twice a day, pulled by {ls.tide}, and {fish} come with it.
  {mountain} was a fire mountain once; it has been asleep longer than the {people} have kept {herd}.
  {land} took a long time to make, with water and wind, before anyone was here to watch.
  (cold=1) The ice comes down from the north every year a little less than it used to.
  (highmountains=1) On the high mountains the air is thin; the {herd} there have bigger hearts than ours.
  (geysers=1) The hot springs are water that went down into the world and came back warm.
  (saltflats=1) The salt flats were a lake. When it dried, it left everything that was in it.
  (archipelago=1) Our islands are the tops of mountains; the rest of them is under the sea.
  (karst=1) The stone towers are what the rain left when it dissolved the rest.
  (glowflora=1) The lamp trees glow to call the insects at night. We moved here partly for them.
  (redsoil=1) The earth is red because of the iron in it. Our oldest pots are the same colour.
  The {tree} on {mountain} are the same kind our ancestors cut for {material}, and they grew back.
  The water in {river} has been the same water, going round, since before there were {people}.
  Under the {fut.green} are the old fields; dig and you will find {crop} seeds from a thousand years ago.
  (coast=1) The sea took the old harbour; on a calm day you can see its {walls.of} walls under the water, and {fish} in them.

# what lives with them
ls.life (share=0.6)
  The {crop} we eat was once a wild grass. [Someone, a long time ago, saved the seeds.|The first {people} found it by the water.|It took a thousand years to make it what it is.]
  The {herd} came here with the first {people}, long before the machines.
  The {herd} in the pens are the same beasts our ancestors kept; only the pens have changed.
  The {bird} on the roofs were here before the city, and they think of the {fut.green} as theirs.
  {fish} still come up {river} in {season.now}, though nobody fishes for them now.
  The {fruit} in the {fut.green} grew wild on {mountain} once, and one of you found one there last year.
  {pest} have eaten our {crop} for as long as there have been records. They are very patient.
  The oldest {tree.n} at {$home} was planted the year the {people} began counting years.
  The {game} in the hills were hunted once. Now they come down to the {fut.gate} to be fed.
  {crop2} came from the far side of the world, a long time ago, in somebody's pocket.
  The {beast} that lives in your house is descended from the ones the first {people} kept.
  The {veg} in your bowl tonight were grown from seed kept by forty generations.
  The {bird} sing a different song here from the ones at {city2}; the old ones said they had quarrelled.

# how they live
ls.ways (share=0.5)
  {festival} is the oldest thing we do. Nobody remembers why we started.
  We keep {feast.custom} because our grandparents did; that is a good enough reason.
  Our houses have {roofs} because {ls.roofwhy}.
  We paint {motif.over} over our doors, every family's a little different; look at yours tonight.
  {colour.holy} is our colour for {ls.colourfor}. Other peoples chose other colours.
  The old {people} made {craft} their pride, and a few of us still do it the slow way, with {material}.
  We count in {measure.far} when we walk, though the machines count in something finer.
  When someone dies we say they have {death.went}. It is an old way of saying it, and a kind one.
  The number {#number} was holy to the old {people}; you will find it everywhere in the old buildings.
  {instrument} is the oldest instrument we have. Its sound has not changed.
  We greet each other with the old words: {blessing}. Say it to someone today.
  The old {people} would swear {oath}, and they kept their oaths, mostly.
  We wear {colour.mourn} for the dead; other peoples wore other colours, and some none at all.
  We play {fut.game} the same way the children of the first {people} played it, with the same arguments.
  The {~word} for a guest is the same as the {~word} for a gift. Remember that.
  Our feast of {festival} once lasted nine days; now it lasts one, and we are just as tired after, and so are the {herd}.
ls.roofwhy
  the old {people} built them so
  of {weather}
  our grandparents liked them
  nobody has thought of a better kind
ls.colourfor
  the dead
  weddings
  the gods
  the first day of the year
  luck
ls.lean
  toward {sun.first}
  away from {sun.first}
ls.tide
  {moon}
  {sun.first}
  the moons

# their words, their past
ls.word
  Our old word for home was {~home}, and some of the old ones in the {fut.block} still use it.
  The word {~river} meant both a river and a life, and we still say {~river} at funerals.
  In the old speech, {~rain} was a kind of blessing; in a dry year the old ones still say {~rain} instead of thank you.
  The children of the first {people} called the moon {~moon}; try it, it is a good word.
  Our word for the {herd} is still {~herd}, though the machines have a dozen better ones.
  The old {people} said {~goodbye} when they parted, which means something like may your {crop} stand.
ls.past (share=0.5)
  Our people are the {people}, who counted their years from {city} and their wealth in {herd}.
  {city} was founded by {founder}, or so the oldest story says, with a cart of {crop} and a {beast}.
  The {people} were few once, then many, then fewer, and the {herd} with them.
  (has:era) We call these years {era}, a name given by people who were not there to see the {fut.green}.
  There were {people} here before there were records; we know them by their stones and their {vessel}.

# the lesson's moral
ls.moral
  {proverb}, as the old ones said.
  The lesson, if there is one: {virtue} outlasts {vice}.
  Life is {life.is}, the old ones said, and on this one I think they were right.
  We are what is left of all of that, with the {crop} and the {herd}, and it is plenty.
  The old {people} would have said: {proverb}.

# the end of a lesson: questions, homework, what the children asked
ls.ask
  {ls.q}
  For next time: {ls.homework}.
  The children asked {ls.childq}. I said {ls.answer}
  One of the children asked {ls.childq}. {ls.noanswer}
  {ls.q} {ls.q2}
ls.q
  (1) {ls.q.plain}
  {ls.q.own}
  {ls.q.own}
  {ls.q.own}
ls.q.plain (share=0.35)
  Questions: why do you think they did it? Would you have?
  Questions: what would you have taken with you?
  Questions: who do you think wrote it down, and why?
  Questions: which of you would have stayed?
  Questions: who was right? Were both?
  Questions: what is one thing we do that they would not understand?
ls.q.own (share=0.8)
  Questions: what would you have planted: {crop}, or {crop2}?
  Questions: what would {founder} make of our {fut.green}?
  Questions: would they have kept the {herd}, if they had had our {fut.green}?
  Questions: what would you have asked {founder}, if you had been there?
  Questions: what do you think they ate, that day? Was it {food}?
  Questions: why do you think they wore {colour.mourn} for it, and not {colour.any}?
  Questions: what would the old {craft} workshops have made of it?
  Questions: what did they do with the {herd} while it happened?
  Questions: what would you have carried out of {$home}, if you could carry one thing?
  Questions: who would you have trusted, and why?
  Questions: what song would they have sung afterwards? Do we still sing it?
  Questions: if you had been there, would you have kept {festival} that year?
ls.q2 (share=0.8)
  Think about it over {food}.
  Ask your grandparents; they will tell you more than they should.
  There is no right answer, only better ones.
  We will talk about it at {festival}.
  Write it on the wall of the {fut.common} if you like; I will read it there.
  Ask in the {craft} workshop; they have opinions about everything there.
  Take your time. It took them a hundred years.
  Answer in a song if you prefer; one of you always does.
  Do not ask the machines. They will only tell you the answer.
  Bring your answer to the {fut.green} tomorrow, before the bell.
ls.homework (share=0.8)
  find one thing in the archive about it, and bring it
  ask the oldest person you know what they were told
  draw {motif.over} from your own door, from memory
  learn the {festival} song, the long version
  find out what your family's {craft} was
  (has:$home.landmark) walk to {$home.landmark} and tell me what is there now
  count the {bird} on your roof at dawn
  find the oldest {tree.n} you can, and sit under it
  learn one word of the old speech, and bring it
  ask the keepers of the {herd} what the {herd} were for
  find {colour.holy} anywhere in {$home}, and tell me where
ls.noanswer (share=0.6)
  I did not have a good answer.
  I said I would ask in the {craft} workshop, where they know everything.
  I told them what my teacher told me, which was nothing, kindly.
  I said: ask me again when the {bird} come back, and I will have thought.
  We sat with it until the bell, which is an answer of sorts.
  I said the old saying, {proverb}, and they groaned, as I did.
  I said it was a question for {holyplace}, not for school.
ls.childq
  (1) {ls.childq.plain}
  {ls.childq.own}
  {ls.childq.own}
ls.childq.plain (share=0.3)
  why we remember sad things
  whether it could happen again
  if it was a long time ago or a very long time ago
  why nobody fixed it
ls.childq.own (share=0.85)
  whether the {bird} saw it
  what the {herd} ate that winter
  why it is not in the {festival} songs
  whether they still had {fruit} then
  what colour they wore for it, and whether it was {colour.mourn}
  whether the {craft} workshop was there yet
  if the {fut.green} were there, or only fields of {crop}
  whether {god} minded
ls.answer (share=0.8)
  that they might know in the {craft} workshop, and to ask there.
  that the old ones would say {proverb}, and that this was not an answer either.
  that we would find out together, which is what teachers say.
  that I would think about it at {festival}.
  to ask the archive, or the {craft} workshop.
  that I did not know, and that it was a good question.
  that it was a very long time ago, and also last week, in a way.

# ---- the assembly's vote -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.vote (words=28..112)
  {vt.head}\n{vt.question}\n{vt.for}\n{vt.against}\n{vt.result}
  {vt.head}\n{vt.question}\n{vt.debate}\n{vt.for}\n{vt.against}\n{vt.result}
  {vt.head}\n{vt.question}\n{vt.against}\n{vt.for}\n{vt.result}

vt.head (formula people=3)
  The assembly of {$seat}, cycle {#year:digits}: the record of the vote.
  Recorded by the archive: a vote of the people of {$home}.
  A vote at {$home}, {season.now}, cycle {#year:digits}.
  The people of {$home} decide. Cycle {#year:digits}.
  Record of the {#n:ord} vote of the cycle, {$seat}.
)VG"
